.TH BYTES 1
.SH NAME
bytes \- display or patch a byte range of standard input
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bytes
.I start end
.RI [ replacement ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
With two arguments,
.I bytes
copies standard input to standard output, printing bytes
.I start
through
.I end
(exclusive) as C-style escapes
.RB ( \e XX
for a non-printable byte,
.B \e\e
for a literal backslash) rather than raw binary \(em a quick way to
inspect a byte range without a hex dump tool.
.PP
With a third argument,
.I bytes
instead copies standard input to standard output with the bytes in
.RI [ start , end )
replaced by
.IR replacement ,
which may itself contain
.BR \en , \er , \et , \ev , \e\e
or
.B \eXX
hex escapes.
.PP
Either
.I start
or
.I end
may be given as the literal word
.B end
to mean the end of the input.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/bytes.b
